[Music]  California's new governor faces backlash  from families of crime victims for  halting all executions President Trump  tweeted that Governor Gavin Newsom is  defying voters who want to keep the  death penalty supporters say the  governor is helping to end a barbaric  and racist practice that does not deter  crime  there are 737 inmates on death row in  California that is the most of any state  in the nation in 2012 and 2016 voters  rejected a measure to abolish capital  punishment aventure new governor Newsom  is here for his first live national TV  interview since his inauguration in  January welcome governor nice to be here  with you thank you for being here this  decision you said came from an emotional  place tell us what you mean by the where  the largest death row not only in  America but the largest in the Western  Hemisphere we've executed 13 people  since 1973 we've spent over 5 billion  dollars in costs associated with it just  last year we had someone who was  exonerated that spent 26 years on death  row joining the ranks of the hundred and  sixty-four human beings that have been  exonerated that have spent time on death  row it's a racist system you cannot deny  that there's no equal justice in the  system it's a system that is  perpetuating inequality it's a system  that I cannot in good conscience support  remember governors of the last back  straw you sign that death you literally  sign a piece of paper putting another  human being to death and if I executed  someone in California I'd be doing it  every day for the next two years so  answer that charge that this was put  before the voters of California in 2012  2016 they said they want the death  penalty and you by executive order have  essentially done something at odds with  the will of your voters and the voters  also entrusted me I have the right I  have the responsibility to advance my  judgment pursuant to the Constitution of  the state and I could provide reprieves  so I also have that right and that's  interested in me by the voters of  California and again this is not an  abstract issue any longer  we have 25 people that have already  cleared and ready to go they've gone  through all their appeals they've been  exhausted we're starting to stack folks  up I want to repeat what I said 737  people  you're gonna execute seven and thirty  seven people it's not only one a day for  two years it could be one a week for 14  years what kind of state what kind of  country does that and by the way that's  a worthy question to answer not just to  ask Saudi Arabia North Korea I mean  there are a few countries in the world  that still execute their citizens in a  premeditated way I don't think the  United States should be continued to be  in that let me ask you about another  topic and that's the environment you  were once called America's greenest  mayor what do you make of the Democrats  new proposal for the green New Deal is  there substance there is there something  to run on in this election yeah I mean  it's the California Dream Act we've been  on it for ten years there's nothing in  the green new deal at California is not  already advancing as our economy is  growing we are radically changing the  way we're producing and consuming energy  California is a leader in the movement  we're the only fully functioning  cap-and-trade program in the United  States and we're creating opportunities  and jobs innovation I love the spirit of  the green New Deal yeah the Republicans  are ripping it apart yeah they're making  stuff up it says as technologically  feasible I mean there's a trigger in the  entire piece of legislation that says  you know and it just it's it's  hyperbolic the fact is if you want any  evidence of climate change being real  come to California the hots are getting  hotter the wets are getting wetter the  dries are getting drier this historic  wildfires mudslides it is very real it's  very raw and in our state Democrats and  Republicans are saying you know what  enough enough we're not interested abate  we want to focus on what to do can we  talk about that college scandal because  two of your schools UCLA and USC where  parents were rigging the system getting  kids in when you heard that governor  what did you think and how were they  able to pull this off for so long you  know what I thought I thought it was  outrageous possible I serve on the UC  regents CSU Board of Trustees yeah but  then I immediately thought this what  about the legalized version of that what  about the folks riding the twenty  million dollar check putting their name  on that building why don't you connect  the dots to the folks that they quietly  called  for admissions or wrote a letter of  recommendation or family members that  got in and I think about how weighted  this is to people with wealth and  privilege not just the folks that are  now being indicted folks that are being  arrested so what's the answer what  I think there's a deep thought we've got  to reflect on this and in much deeper  way it's all about money it's all about  money  two-thirds of folks are in these  positions of influence as administrators  their purpose they're literally judged  on their basis of their connections and  their ability to raise money but with  that comes some expectation people  aren't just benignly riding a hundred  million dollar checks yeah they expect  something more than just their name  attached to it they're gonna get a  return phone call that to me is a more  insidious issue that we need to address  is that truth politicians that's deeply  true of politicians deeply - if anyone  in positions of influence and  politicians are among them but we see it  all the time as celebrities we see it  with people again with means they're  making calls to folks like me they don't  even need to make calls folks like me  because they've accessed the folks that  are making the real decisions within the  bureaucracies well governor Newsom I  know you're it's nice to have you here  in New York or in California let's go  back again honored thank you

